  "prompt_text": "This figure gives a broad landscape-to-ocean view of the carbon cycle and emphasizes how microbes transform carbon across connected environments. Carbon dioxide from the atmosphere and industry is shown moving into land and water systems, while sunlight, heat, rain, and soil type appear as major controls. On land, carbon passes through tundra, agriculture, forests, wetlands, estuaries, and semiarid scrub, with inputs from plant material such as root exudates, leaves, and dead wood. Microbial decomposition and detritivores break this material down, and respiration returns CO₂ to the atmosphere.\n\nIn the ocean, the diagram highlights microbial primary production in the photic zone, photosynthesis, deposition to deeper waters, and transfer into microbial biomass. Oxygen-minimum zones and deep-sea sediments are included, suggesting that carbon processing continues below the surface through remineralization and decomposition. Coastal habitats such as mangroves, coral, lakes, and estuaries connect terrestrial and marine carbon pathways.\n\nThe figure also contrasts different timescales: rapid industrial CO₂ release versus very slow burial into fossil fuels. In soils and sediments, microbial activity can produce both CO₂ and CH₄, especially through methanogenesis, showing that microbes are central to whether carbon is stored, recycled, or emitted.",
